S7: keep mDNS to the LAN, bounded, and stoppable

The mDNS code is open to anyone on the link, and upstream trusts it:

- The daemons listen and announce on every interface, including a
  mobile-data or VPN interface with a public address.
- Discovery connects a TCP socket to every address a service announces,
  one at a time, with no timeout, to see whether it answers: any host on
  the link can make the phone open connections to addresses of its
  choosing, and stall discovery for the OS's connect timeout on each.
- Every resolved service is cached without limit.
- When the daemon's channel fails, discovery logs and loops, and since
  the channel never yields again it spins a core at 100 %.
- Stopping waits for the goodbye with a blocking `recv()` on the async
  runtime, which stalls a runtime thread for as long as the daemon takes
  (forever if it is gone), and the daemon thread itself is never shut
  down.
- The announced device name is written with a one-byte length (`as u8`)
  and, in the connection request, cut at 255 bytes wherever that falls:
  a longer name wraps the length or splits a character and makes the
  whole record unreadable for peers. The receiving parser also indexes
  the record by hand.

Both daemons now take an AddrFilter, the embedding application's reach
policy (Sukkula's S7: private, link-local and unique-local addresses
only): they run only on IPv4 interfaces whose address it accepts, and
discovery reports only services at an accepted address, which nothing
probes. At most 64 services are remembered, a failed channel ends
discovery, the goodbye and the daemon shutdown are awaited without
blocking and for at most half a second each, and dropping either type
shuts its daemon down too. Announced names are cut to 128 bytes on a
character boundary, and the endpoint-info parser uses bounds-checked
reads. The service type is one constant.

Findings: S7. Report: docs/UPSTREAM-QUICKSHARE.md R12.
